<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Supplementary Fundamental Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1907</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Supplementary Fundamental Law was the second part of the Persian Constitution, ratified in October 1907 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>. Modelled partly on the Belgian constitution, it set out civil rights, equality before the law and the separation of powers, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> the state religion, with a committee of senior clerics to check that laws conformed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. It remained in force, at least formally, until 1979.</p></div>
